          "text": "The pattern is **not consistent with a single Hf phase**. The most securely identified component is hexagonal **\u03c9-Hf**, based on the characteristic peak pair near 36.9\u201337.5\u00b0 and the reflections at 29.73, 53.5, 61.7, and 80.5\u00b0. Hexagonal \u03b1-Hf and probably a small amount of \u03b2-Hf are also present.\n\nSeveral very intense peaks\u2014notably 18.50, 26.02, 28.31, and 40.31\u00b0\u2014remain unexplained by these phases. Many can be indexed using a complex primitive-cubic cell with \\(a\\approx14.09\\) \u00c5, but its Hf atomic arrangement is not uniquely recoverable from this one-dimensional pattern. Thus the following primary CIF should be regarded as the most secure identified phase, not a complete whole-pattern solution.\n\n### Primary candidate: \u03c9-Hf\n\n```cif\ndata_Hf_omega\n_audit_creation_method              'Indexed from supplied Cu Kalpha1 powder pattern'\n_chemical_name_common               'omega-hafnium'\n_chemical_formula_structural        Hf\n_chemical_formula_sum               'Hf3'\n\n_cell_length_a                      4.793\n_cell_length_b                      4.793\n_cell_length_c                      3.003\n_cell_angle_alpha                   90\n_cell_angle_beta                    90\n_cell_angle_gamma                   120\n_cell_volume                        59.73\n\n_space_group_name_H-M_alt           'P 6/m m m'\n_space_group_IT_number              191\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_symmetry_multiplicity\n_atom_site_Wyckoff_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\nHf1 Hf 1 a 0.000000 0.000000 0.000000 1.0\nHf2 Hf 2 d 0.333333 0.666667 0.500000 1.0\n```\n\nDiagnostic \u03c9-Hf assignments include approximately: 001 at 29.73\u00b0, 101 at 36.9\u00b0, 110 at 37.5\u00b0, 201 near 53.5\u00b0, 002 at 61.7\u00b0, 202 near 78.1\u00b0, and 220 near 80.5\u00b0.\n\n### Surviving secondary phase: \u03b1-Hf\n\n```cif\ndata_Hf_alpha\n_chemical_name_common               'alpha-hafnium'\n_chemical_formula_structural        Hf\n_chemical_formula_sum               'Hf2'\n\n_cell_length_a                      3.179\n_cell_length_b                      3.179\n_cell_length_c                      5.055\n_cell_angle_alpha                   90\n_cell_angle_beta                    90\n_cell_angle_gamma                   120\n_cell_volume                        44.24\n\n_space_group_name_H-M_alt           'P 63/m m c'\n_space_group_IT_number              194\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_symmetry_multiplicity\n_atom_site_Wyckoff_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\nHf1 Hf 2 c 0.333333 0.666667 0.250000 1.0\n```\n\nThis accounts for the reflections near 32.51\u00b0 (100), 35.48\u00b0 (002), approximately 37.2\u00b0 (101), 49\u00b0 (102), 57.95\u00b0 (110), and 68.08\u00b0 (200).\n\n### Possible minor phase: \u03b2-Hf\n\n```cif\ndata_Hf_beta\n_chemical_name_common               'beta-hafnium'\n_chemical_formula_structural        Hf\n_chemical_formula_sum               'Hf2'\n\n_cell_length_a                      3.538\n_cell_length_b                      3.538\n_cell_length_c                      3.538\n_cell_angle_alpha                   90\n_cell_angle_beta                    90\n_cell_angle_gamma                   90\n_cell_volume                        44.29\n\n_space_group_name_H-M_alt           'I m -3 m'\n_space_group_IT_number              229\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_symmetry_multiplicity\n_atom_site_Wyckoff_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\nHf1 Hf 2 a 0.000000 0.000000 0.000000 1.0\n```\n\nA unique complete CIF for the dominant unidentified component would require either additional diffraction data or the candidate structural model/database from which the complex \\(a\\approx14.09\\) \u00c5 cell was generated.",
